'''Ubax Sago''' (née '''Koofur'''; born 5 April 1985) is a [[Burawa|Burawi]] politician who has served as the commune representative for '''Dafar Sare-1''' in the [[Dafar Sare Rural Administration]] since 2009. She is a member of the [[Sago Revolutionary Party]] (SRP) and the wife of [[Bile Sago]], the party's founder and senator for the Dafar Sare Rural Administration.

Ubax Sago is a niece of Halgan Koofur, Bile Sago's first wife, who died in the [[Kedam train derailment]] in 1985.

== Early life and education ==

Ubax Koofur was born on 5 April 1985 in Ead, a town in the [[Sago]] area of western [[Burawa]]. She is of half [[Burawa|Burawi]] and half [[Balisca|Baliscan]] descent. Koofur qualified for the '''Continued Education Programme''', a track within the [[Burawi Work Programme]] that permits eligible students to proceed directly to university in lieu of a service assignment. She studied economics at the [[University of Sago]].

Koofur had known [[Bile Sago]] for the entirety of her life through her family's connection to him — her aunt, Halgan Koofur, had been Sago's first wife. She worked on Sago's political campaigns from an early stage of his career in the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]].

== Political career ==

=== Love Tour and election ===

In 2009, Koofur stood as a candidate for the commune representative seat of '''Dafar Sare-1''' alongside Bile Sago's simultaneous campaign for the [[Dafar Sare Rural Administration]] Senate seat. The joint campaign, branded the '''"Love Tour"''' ({{lang|bur|''Safarka Jacaylka''}}), was built around a public promise that the couple would marry if both won. Both candidates were elected, and the couple married on 8 December 2009.

The couple's eldest son, [[Guled Sago]], had been born in 2006, prior to the marriage.

=== National Party years ===

Ubax Sago served as the commune representative for Dafar Sare-1 as a member of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] from 2009 to 2020. During this period, she maintained her party affiliation through her husband's expulsion from the National Party in 2016 and his readmission in 2018. Her position within the party was not affected by Bile Sago's controversies.

=== Sago Revolutionary Party ===

When Bile Sago founded the [[Sago Revolutionary Party]] in 2020, Ubax Sago left the National Party and joined the SRP. She has been re-elected to the Dafar Sare-1 commune seat under the SRP banner.

=== Coalition building ===

In contrast to her husband's confrontational public style, Ubax Sago has assumed a role focused on building working relationships between the SRP and the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] at the local level. Her continued connections within the National Party — in which she served for over a decade — have facilitated cooperation on constituency matters in the Dafar Sare Rural Administration.

== Personal life ==

Ubax Sago was born Ubax Koofur in Ead, [[Sago]]. She is a niece of Halgan Koofur, the first wife of [[Bile Sago]], who died in the [[Kedam train derailment]] of 1985 — the year of Ubax's birth.

She married Bile Sago on 8 December 2009. The couple have two children. Their eldest son, [[Guled Sago]] (born 2006), is a political commentator.

